I like that its a small patch and likely does fix the issue. I do wonder though if we're going to be working around functional sensor drivers with this. How would this work with a correctly written submit call that *is* asynchronous as would be expected? How do we help guide sensor developers with this change to do the right thing?

My proposition here would be instead to create a little struct helper functions to do this inside the driver instead. Akin to the i2c_rtio_context perhaps. There could be both a p4wq sensor context as well in the future as the more ideal non-blocking context which could still provide some common code structure for things.

E.g.

``` c 
struct sensor_rtio_p4wq_context {
   struct k_p4wq_work work;
   struct rtio_iodev_sqe *sqe;
};
```

Then in say.. bme280 submit

```c
void bme280_blocking_submit(...)
{
  i2c_transfer(...); /* blocking call */

  rtio_iodev_sqe_ok(..);
}

void bme280_submit(struct rtio_iodev_sqe *sqe) 
{
    struct bme280_data *data = dev->data;

    sensor_rtio_p4wq_submit(data->sensor_ctx, sqe);
}
```


Does that make sense? I could be wrong!
